BY DESIGN, NOT BY MERIT. This directory is the CONTROL arm of
test-defcon2-holdfire-contact. Its rules.yaml differs from the treatment's by exactly one
quantity -- DefconEscalation.ModeDefault, Escalation -> Skirmish -- and
DefconEscalationState's constructor answers that with `Level = NoLevel` (0) outright, so
DefconFireDiscipline.HoldsFire(0) is false and NOTHING on this map is holding fire.

Two enemy tanks on FireAtWill, ten cells apart, mutually in range on an unfogged map, engage
each other within a couple of autotarget scan intervals (AutoTargetInfo.Min/MaximumScanTime-
Interval are 3 and 8 ticks). The shared body's hold-window assertion then fails on whichever
of its four observables moves first -- ammunition, health, Test.GetImpactEffectCount, or the
automatic-coloured target line. FAIL is the only outcome this arm can honestly produce.

WHY IT EXISTS. A green treatment is not evidence unless something could have made it red
(AUTOTEST.md, "A green run is not evidence unless something could have made it RED"). The
recorded failure of that rule is test-autotarget-preempt-air, where BOTH required arms passed
and the fix had already shipped on the strength of the green one. This arm is what makes the
treatment's silence mean "the rule held" rather than "nothing was ever going to fire here".

A PASS HERE IS LOUDLY RED AND FALSIFIES THE TREATMENT, NOT THIS FILE. It would mean two
enemy tanks in mutual weapon range on FireAtWill did not engage each other in an ordinary
Skirmish -- so the geometry, the stances, the ownership or the targeting is wrong in BOTH
directories and the treatment's green was measuring nothing. Report and stop; do not move the
window or re-run hoping for the other answer.

THE ONE FAILURE THIS DECLARATION MUST NOT ABSORB, because it produces the declared outcome
for the wrong reason: a setup fault. The shared body fails at SETUP rather than in the hold
window when the pair is not a live targeting pair (Test.GetTargetOrder does not resolve to
Attack), when the separation is outside either weapon envelope, or when the match opens at
DEFCON 3 or 1. Those verdicts all begin `fail: SETUP` and they are staging faults in BOTH
arms. Read the reason string; a `fail: SETUP` here is not this arm doing its job.

The verdict line to expect carries `defcon=0` in its census. If it reads `defcon=2` the
override did not merge and this directory is a second copy of the treatment -- which today
would fail too, and two identical arms both failing looks exactly like a clean result.

REMOVE THIS FILE only if the control legitimately starts passing, which would mean the map
can no longer separate the two hypotheses and needs new geometry rather than a new
declaration. A hang, a crash, or a run that never reaches a verdict still reds under it: a
declaration buys silence for the one outcome it names and nothing else.
